# Overpeaks Launcher Mobile

A mobile-first 3D launcher prototype inspired by Minecraft Bedrock-style item placement, NPC interaction, and world persistence. This project is designed to be built in Unity for Android and works fully offline.

This repository contains a Unity project scaffold and runtime code to bootstrap a playable prototype directly in the Unity editor. It is intended for an Android mobile game concept and is optimized for devices like the Realme 33.

## Included

- Android-ready Unity project structure
- Offline local world save system
- World generator with block placement and removal
- Inventory system with item types and item placement
- NPC interaction and basic AI
- Launcher/bootstrapping flow that creates a playable scene automatically
- Documentation for building an APK

## Important note

This repository does not include a final packaged APK because Unity builds require the Unity editor and Android SDK installed locally. The project is set up so you can open it in Unity 2022.3 LTS, switch to Android, and build a signed APK from the project.

## Current project status

Prototype is in place and designed for a full 3D mobile version. It is intentionally lightweight and optimized so it can be expanded with more NPC behaviors, quests, world generation, and custom asset packs.

## Recommended Unity setup

- Unity Hub
- Unity 2022.3 LTS
- Android Build Support (Android SDK + JDK)
- Android device or emulator for test runs

## Quick start

1. Download and install Unity Hub + Unity 2022.3 LTS.
2. Open this repository as a Unity project.
3. In Unity, open the project and accept the default package install.
4. In Build Settings, select Android.
5. Create a scene by enabling the auto-bootstrap script (it runs automatically on project open in a blank scene).
6. Press Play in the editor, or build to APK.

## Offline mode

Everything is stored in the app's local persistent data path:

- world save files
- NPC state
- item inventory
- settings

No external server is required.

## Project structure

- `Assets/Scripts/Runtime` - main gameplay scripts
- `Packages/manifest.json` - Unity package dependencies
- `ProjectSettings` - Unity project settings

## Example gameplay loop

- Open app
- Bootstraps a world and player
- Move with keyboard or touch input
- Place/remove blocks
- Use collected items
- Interact with NPCs near the player
- Save world state locally

